# Multi-Documentation Hub and Forum Documentation View

## Context

`integrate/` is a standalone Vite documentation reference with a fixed,
compile-time page registry, grouped sidebar, responsive documentation shell,
search dialog, article renderer, on-page headings, and previous/next
navigation. Its HrtmPay/Frappe content, branding, Markdown import pipeline,
React router shell, remote fonts, and hardcoded theme tokens do not belong in
Skaia.

Skaia already has a full HTML TipTap editor in
`frontend/src/components/forum/Editor.tsx`, sanitized read-only rendering in
`frontend/src/components/ui/RichTextRenderer.tsx`, and forum grid, list, and
traditional article-feed modes. It does not have tenant-owned documentation
models, APIs, navigation state, or routes. The integration therefore needs one
shared documentation presentation shell with two deliberately separate data
adapters:

- `/doc` reads and writes new documentation-domain records through its own
  handler, service, and repositories.
- `/forum/docs` projects existing forum categories and threads into the same
  presentation shell. Editing there updates the authoritative forum thread;
  it never copies forum content into documentation tables.

## Goal

- Add a public multi-documentation hub where a tenant can host any number of
  independently named documentation sets.
- Use canonical public routes:
  - `/doc` - documentation catalog and creation entrypoint for authorized users.
  - `/doc/:documentationSlug` - one documentation overview or its first
    visible guide.
  - `/doc/:documentationSlug/:articleSlug` - one guide/article.
- Let an authorized owner create, rename, reorder, update, and retire sidebar
  sections and guides from the documentation shell using Skaia's full TipTap
  HTML editor and sanitized renderer.
- Add `/forum/docs`, `/forum/docs/:categoryId`, and
  `/forum/docs/:categoryId/:threadId` as the forum documentation layout, with
  forum categories as sidebar sections and threads as articles.
- Adapt the useful `integrate/` UX to Skaia's global layout, branding, theme,
  permissions, lifecycle, realtime, security, and responsive contracts.

## Non-Goals

- Do not import the HrtmPay/Frappe articles, source links, brand assets, remote
  fonts, browser-storage registry, or build/runtime dependencies from
  `integrate/`.
- Do not store documentation content in `pages`, forum tables, Markdown files,
  or site-config JSON.
- Do not mirror forum categories or threads into documentation records or make
  the documentation service mutate forum records.
- Do not replace the existing forum grid, list, article feed, thread details,
  comments, likes, or canonical `/view-thread/:threadId` routes.
- Do not add collaborative cursors, approval workflows, article version
  history, localization/i18n infrastructure, arbitrary custom CSS, or a second
  rich-text editor implementation in this delivery.
- Do not impose a fixed documentation-count limit. Existing request-size,
  rate-limit, storage, and authorization controls still apply.

## Contracts

### Documentation ownership and persistence

- Add migration `036_documentation_hub.sql` with lifecycle-aware tables:
  - `documentations`: tenant-local unique active slug, title, description,
    owner, visibility, timestamps, and `deleted_at`/`deleted_by`.
  - `documentation_sections`: documentation parent, title, stable display
    order, timestamps, and lifecycle columns.
  - `documentation_articles`: documentation and optional section parent,
    unique active `(documentation_id, slug)`, title, summary, TipTap HTML
    content, display order, author/last editor, optimistic revision or
    `updated_at` token, timestamps, and lifecycle columns.
- Use foreign keys and partial active indexes. A retired parent hides its
  descendants without runtime hard deletion; register all three resource types
  with the existing lifecycle audit, hard-delete guard, and Trash restore
  policy. Reordering must be transactional and collision-safe.
- The creator owns a documentation. Owners may manage their own documentation;
  `docs.manage` may manage any documentation and `docs.create` controls new
  documentation creation. Seed permissions idempotently for admin/superuser.
  Public/unlisted reads never confer write access. Private documentation reads
  require the same owner/manage authorization as writes.
- Normalize and validate slugs server-side, reserve route-control names such as
  `mine`, require non-empty bounded titles/content, and return `409` for active
  slug or stale-revision conflicts. Slug changes return the authoritative new
  route so clients replace history rather than leaving stale URLs.

### Documentation backend domain and API

- Add `backend/internal/documentation` with the normal
  `handler => service => repository` boundary, domain interfaces, focused
  repository/service/handler tests, cache invalidation, event records, and
  WebSocket dispatch. Documentation and forum repositories remain separate.
- Mount `/api/docs` in `backend/main.go`. Register literal management routes
  before slug routes. The API includes:
  - public visible-document listing, one documentation navigation manifest,
    one article read, and bounded search within one documentation;
  - authenticated owned-document listing and documentation create/update/
    retire operations;
  - section create/update/retire and one transactional navigation reorder;
  - article create/update/retire using numeric IDs for mutations even though
    public reads use slugs.
- Manifests return documentation metadata plus ordered section/article summary
  records, not every article body. Article reads return one body and adjacent
  navigation metadata. Search derives bounded sanitized plain-text excerpts
  and never interpolates raw HTML or unescaped SQL patterns.
- API responses remain `no-store`; Redis entries are best-effort,
  tenant-prefixed, and invalidated after successful writes. The mutation
  response is authoritative for its sender.
- Add a `documentation:update` WebSocket message plus documentation/article
  resource subscriptions. Push compact create/update/delete/reorder
  invalidations and refetch matching manifests/articles; do not broadcast full
  HTML bodies. Preserve a locally dirty editor and surface a conflict instead
  of silently overwriting it.

### Shared documentation shell

- Build a route-scoped Skaia shell inspired by `integrate/`, not a file copy:
  responsive sidebar groups, documentation switcher/back-to-catalog affordance,
  breadcrumbs, search, title/summary metadata, copy-link action, generated
  on-this-page headings, previous/next guide navigation, empty/loading/error
  states, and mobile drawer/scrim focus behavior.
- Keep Skaia's global `Layout`, header, footer, branding, `SiteHead`, and theme
  authority. The documentation shell must account for existing chrome and must
  not mount another `BrowserRouter`, global reset, remote font, or competing
  root theme variables.
- Use shared primitives, Lucide icons, CSS tokens, light/dark mode, reduced
  motion, semantic landmarks, keyboard navigation, visible focus, and the
  current phone-width TipTap contract. No native dialogs or hardcoded CSS
  colors.
- Extract a reusable editor boundary from the forum editor only as needed so
  forum and documentation use one extension registry, HTML output contract,
  import behavior, lazy loading, toolbar, and media/upload rules. Read mode uses
  `RichTextRenderer`; never use `rehypeRaw` or a second unsanitized renderer.
- Inline edit mode is permission-gated and has explicit Edit, Save, Cancel,
  saving, validation, stale-conflict, and unsaved-navigation states. Save sends
  the last observed revision. Cancel restores the server snapshot. Creation and
  reorder controls must remain usable by keyboard and touch.
- Generate heading anchors from the sanitized rendered document with stable
  duplicate-heading suffixes, preserve authored safe IDs when valid, and keep
  hash navigation/copy links correct after edits.

### Documentation data adapter and routes

- Add a focused Jotai/API layer for documentation manifests, selected article,
  search results, edit drafts, loading/error state, and realtime invalidation.
  Persist only explicitly recoverable drafts or view preferences, scoped by
  documentation/article ID and user; do not persist whole public manifests or
  server content as authority.
- Add lazy public routes for `/doc`, `/doc/:documentationSlug`, and
  `/doc/:documentationSlug/:articleSlug`. Management controls appear inline
  only after current-user permissions are known; the backend remains the
  authority.
- `/doc` always supports multiple documentation sets. One existing set must not
  silently change the route contract. A documentation with no articles renders
  an owner creation state and a public empty state; a missing/private resource
  does not leak existence.
- Add `docs` to Skaia's feature registry/CLI defaults and navigation visibility
  rules. A disabled feature removes shared-navigation entrypoints and resolves
  public doc routes consistently with other disabled modules.

### Forum documentation adapter

- Add a forum-owned lightweight documentation manifest/search projection that
  returns ordered category metadata and thread summaries without loading all
  thread bodies or issuing a request per category. It lives under
  `/api/forum`, uses forum repositories/services, existing public-read rules,
  active-row filters, cache behavior, and optional-user permission enrichment.
- Map category `display_order` and pin state into sidebar ordering; map threads
  to guide links using stable numeric IDs. Fetch the selected thread and its
  comments/actions through existing forum APIs and atoms. Locked categories or
  threads are readable but retain their existing mutation restrictions.
- `/forum/docs` is an additional route-linked view reachable from the forum
  view controls. It does not replace the current `articles` feed. Deep links
  select the category/thread, hard refresh correctly, and canonicalize a moved
  thread to its current category without duplicating content.
- The shell's forum Edit action opens inline TipTap for users whose authoritative
  thread data says `can_edit`; Save calls the existing
  `PUT /api/forum/threads/{id}` mutation with title, content, and category.
  Existing `forum:update`, notifications, analytics, mentions, last-editor
  metadata, cache invalidation, comments, likes, locks, and resource
  subscriptions remain authoritative.
- Forum search results and sidebar links stay in `/forum/docs/...`; ordinary
  forum/thread links elsewhere retain their current routes. The adapter must
  not import documentation atoms, call `/api/docs`, or create documentation
  rows.

### Public discovery and security

- Extend tenant sitemap/SSR metadata resolution for public documentation and
  forum-documentation deep links without caching request-origin data. Retired,
  private, or missing documents/articles are absent/noindex; backend/config
  degradation follows the existing `503` and cacheability contract.
- Sanitize all rendered TipTap HTML through `RichTextRenderer`; retain its
  validated media placeholders, mention handling, link safety, and preview
  behavior. Validate write payload sizes and URL/visibility values server-side.
- Add documentation mutations to events/audit coverage, CSRF-equivalent bearer
  auth assumptions, rate limits, lifecycle/Trash behavior, and tenant cache/SEO
  invalidation. Do not rely on hidden buttons, route obscurity, or guest sandbox
  mode for authorization.

## Steps

1. Confirm the route/model/API contract against current forum, lifecycle,
   permissions, feature, SEO, caching, and WebSocket code; add migration 036,
   documentation models/interfaces/repositories, and migration tests.
2. Implement the documentation service/handler, ownership and visibility
   policy, manifests/articles/search, transactional navigation reorder,
   lifecycle restore behavior, cache/event/WS dispatch, mount, and backend tests.
3. Refactor the existing forum editor into a shared lazy editor boundary without
   changing its serialized HTML/extensions; build the tokenized responsive
   documentation shell, safe heading index, navigation, search dialog, and
   accessibility tests from the `integrate/` behavior inventory.
4. Implement documentation atoms/API adapters, catalog and nested routes,
   inline create/edit/reorder/retire flows, conflict/dirty-draft protection,
   realtime refetch, feature gating, and frontend integration tests.
5. Add the forum-owned manifest/search projection and `/forum/docs/...` adapter;
   wire view selection, deep links, existing thread edit/update/comment/action
   behavior, moved-thread canonicalization, and regression tests proving no
   documentation-table writes occur.
6. Add sitemap/SSR/cache invalidation and navigation entrypoints; run backend,
   frontend, responsive, accessibility, security, lifecycle, and multi-tenant
   verification. Update affected specs and this tip only after implementation
   establishes the final contracts.

## Verification

- Migration/repository tests cover idempotence, active slug uniqueness, same
  article slug in different documentation sets, unbounded set creation without
  a product cap, ordering, parent retirement visibility, restore conflicts,
  ownership, private-resource non-disclosure, and stale revisions.
- Backend: `go test ./...` from `backend/`, including handler/service/repository,
  auth, search bounds, cache invalidation, WebSocket payload, forum projection,
  lifecycle, sitemap, and SSR tests.
- Frontend: focused Vitest suites plus `npm test -- --run`, `npm run lint`,
  `npm run check:native-dialogs`, and `npm run build` from `frontend/`.
- Manual documentation flow: create `example` and `example2`, add/reorder
  sections and guides, duplicate an article slug across the two sets, edit rich
  HTML/media, cancel/save/conflict, copy/hash links, search, previous/next,
  visibility changes, retire/restore, back/forward, and hard refresh at every
  `/doc` route.
- Manual forum flow: open `/forum/docs`, switch categories, search, deep-link a
  thread, edit inline, move it to another category, use comments/likes/locks,
  observe a second-client realtime refresh, and confirm the canonical forum
  view reflects the same updated thread.
- Responsive/accessibility: desktop/tablet/phone, long titles, empty and large
  sidebars, drawer focus trap/return, Escape, keyboard reorder, reduced motion,
  light/dark mode, screen-reader landmarks, and no content hidden behind Skaia's
  global header/footer.
- Separation gate: tests or repository spies prove `/api/docs` uses only
  documentation repositories and `/forum/docs` mutations use only forum
  services. `rg` must find no imported HrtmPay/Frappe corpus, second
  `BrowserRouter`, `rehypeRaw`, remote font import, or duplicate rich-text
  extension registry in the new Skaia paths.
- CSS drift:
  `rg "#[0-9a-fA-F]{3,8}|rgba\\(|rgb\\(|var\\([^)]*," frontend/src --glob '*.css'`.
- Planner closeout verifies all new routes/messages/tables/features are reflected
  in `.specs/backend_spec`, `.specs/frontend_spec`,
  `.specs/route_resolution_spec`, `.specs/realtime_wss_spec`,
  `.specs/caching_cdn_spec`, `.specs/security_architecture_spec`,
  `.specs/migrations_spec`, and `.specs/README.md`.

